## Mode 3: The logistics block

The logistics block is structured data about the crawl. It sits beneath the essay as the terse practical counterpart. Deliberate contrast: the essay has a voice, the logistics block is just facts.

### What it contains

Required items (all crawls):
- **Start:** Nearest tube/rail station to the first pub. Named station, not "central London."
- **End:** Nearest tube/rail station to the last pub.
- **Pubs:** Count.
- **Distance:** Approximate walking distance in miles, from the first pub to the last, following the crawl order. Round to nearest half-mile.
- **Duration:** Realistic total time, including transport between pubs and time in each pub. For drinking-focused crawls (Monopoly), assume 20 min per pub plus transport. For relaxed crawls (Bermondsey), assume longer. State whether most people finish.
- **Best day/time:** When the pubs are open and when it's genuinely enjoyable. Saturday-only venues flagged.

Optional items (where relevant):
- **Known hazards:** Crawl-specific operational advice that catches people out. "The Ten Bells closes its upstairs after 8pm, which is where the Ripper history actually is." "Kernel and Fourpure queue early — go there first." Must be specific. Generic tips are banned.

### Format

Short bullet points. Four to seven items max. No adjectives. No flourishes. No prose.

Good:
> - **Start:** London Bridge (Bermondsey Street exit)
> - **End:** South Bermondsey
> - **Pubs:** 15
> - **Distance:** ~1.5 miles
> - **Duration:** 6-8 hours; most people hit 8-10 venues
> - **Best day:** Saturday 11am-7pm — most taprooms are Saturday-only
> - **Known hazard:** Kernel and Fourpure queue early; start there

Bad (the anti-pattern):
> - **Comfortable shoes:** Most of the route is on foot...
> - **Stay hydrated:** Pace yourself with water breaks...
> - **Eat along the way:** Stick to meal deals...

Every generic tip. If the advice applies to any crawl, it doesn't go here. Only crawl-specific operational advice makes it in.

### Data sources

Most logistics fields can be extracted from the crawl's pub data file (the TypeScript files in `src/content/crawls/`). Specifically:

- **Pub count:** length of the pubs array
- **Distance:** sum of haversine distances between consecutive pubs, plus rough adjustment for real-walking overhead (~1.3x straight-line)
- **Duration:** sum of `transportTime` values + (pub count × 20 min average in each pub)
- **Start/end stations:** nearest tube station to first and last pub coordinates

If generating logistics for a crawl, pull what you can from the data file first, then ask the user to confirm and fill in the "best day" and "known hazards" fields, which are editorial judgments the data doesn't capture.

